WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:03.434
[ Up-tempo theme music plays ]

00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:06.667
-"Circuit breakers don't flip,
they trip."

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:10.534
I got them confused
with break-dancers.

00:00:10.534 --> 00:00:14.734
[ Laughter ]

00:00:14.734 --> 00:00:20.167
"A Toshiba T1100 wouldn't have
the processing power

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.434
to burn your thighs."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:23.434 --> 00:00:27.467
Computers used to be slow,
but they weren't hot as [Bleep].

00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:28.901
[ Laughter ]

00:00:28.901 --> 00:00:31.501
You could play Prince of Persia
for 12 hours,

00:00:31.501 --> 00:00:33.567
and we did.

00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:37.033
Your thighs would be
cool as ice.

00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:38.667
[ Laughter ]

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:40.667
You could store meat on them.

00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:43.434
[ Laughter ]

00:00:43.434 --> 00:00:47.534
I said Whiskers wasn't
a person's last name.

00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:49.234
But some of you did
a little research check,

00:00:49.234 --> 00:00:51.300
checked places
like ancestry.com,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.934
and figured out somewhere
between 700 to 850 people

00:00:54.934 --> 00:00:57.567
currently have that last name.

00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:00.834
I guess it was a common surname
for cake makers.

00:01:00.834 --> 00:01:06.067
I don't have a lot of confidence
about tonight's "Corrections."

00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:08.100
[ Laughter ]

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.901
Joyce did a lot.
Joyce sent in a lot.

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:14.801
And so sometimes I feel like
it's gonna be red-hot,

00:01:14.801 --> 00:01:17.200
and -- Yeah.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.334
Like, if you got a ton
going on today or tonight

00:01:21.334 --> 00:01:24.501
or whenever you're watching it,

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:26.634
you can skip it.

00:01:26.634 --> 00:01:28.667
[ Laughter ]

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:31.601
A lot of people came out
in defense of the Compass app

00:01:31.601 --> 00:01:33.501
on the iPhone.

00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:37.801
Some ecologists said it
comes in very handy for them.

00:01:37.801 --> 00:01:40.000
And a few people said,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.400
"You should be careful
what you say.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.701
If you're lost
in the wilderness,

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:44.734
it's incredibly helpful."
Just make sure it's a wilderness

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:47.567
where all the trees
have outlets.

00:01:47.567 --> 00:01:50.067
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:52.801
Just look,
if you need a compass,

00:01:52.801 --> 00:01:56.334
get a compass.
Get a practical --

00:01:56.334 --> 00:02:00.267
Don't count on
your phone's compass.

00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:02.334
You can't over-rely
on technology.

00:02:02.334 --> 00:02:05.534
I could get a teleprompter,
but I use Wally,

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:09.033
because, for his faults,
never --

00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:12.100
Battery has never gone dead.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.968 --> 00:02:16.634
During H.R. meetings,
goes low -- gets low.

00:02:16.634 --> 00:02:19.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:19.601 --> 00:02:21.968
Definitely been like
somewhere between 3% and 5%

00:02:21.968 --> 00:02:24.334
at a couple of
those H.R. meetings.

00:02:24.334 --> 00:02:26.334
[ Laughter ]

00:02:26.334 --> 00:02:28.033
Speaking of Wally,
I often tell him --

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:29.834
not in the body of the show --

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:32.200
I say, "Stop interrupting
my monologue,"

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.701
because he'll -- he'll talk.
And one of you wrote,

00:02:34.701 --> 00:02:36.734
"It's impossible for him
to interrupt a monologue,

00:02:36.734 --> 00:02:39.267
because as soon as he talks,
it's a dialog."

00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:41.267
This is the [Bleep]...

00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:43.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.601 --> 00:02:45.667
...I got to put up with.

00:02:45.667 --> 00:02:48.767
[ Laughter ]

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:51.267
[ Sighs ]

00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:54.501
Did a "Jokes Seth Can't Tell"
this week.

00:02:54.501 --> 00:02:56.601
A banger, by the way,
worth watching.

00:02:56.601 --> 00:02:59.467
A really good one.
Jackal wrote,

00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:02.067
"You need to change
the graphic of Seth

00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:03.667
in the opening package."
And I immediately thought,

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:06.067
"That's a great point,
because I'm wearing a suit,

00:03:06.067 --> 00:03:08.334
old haircut."
You know what?

00:03:08.334 --> 00:03:12.734
Good work, Jackal.
Actually thought, "Look at that.

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:16.667
A helpful note from a Jackal,"
then kept reading.

00:03:16.667 --> 00:03:19.133
"You need to change the graphic
of Seth in the opening package.

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:21.667
He looks so much older now."

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:24.534
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:27.067
Di-- Distinguished.

00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:30.567
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.567 --> 00:03:32.334
Also in that segment, I joked

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:34.133
that when I blow out
my birthday candles,

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:35.467
I wish for lesbian twins.

00:03:35.467 --> 00:03:38.467
Jenny took offense,
as per uzhe.

00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:40.801
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.801 --> 00:03:43.534
But one of you ran to my defense
and said, "Shame on Jenny."

00:03:43.534 --> 00:03:46.901
I didn't say I wish for lesbian
twins for sexual reasons.

00:03:46.901 --> 00:03:48.701
She just jumped
to that conclusion.

00:03:48.701 --> 00:03:50.334
Maybe I wish for them

00:03:50.334 --> 00:03:53.167
because I needed
some woodworking done

00:03:53.167 --> 00:03:57.334
and wanted to support
a family business.

00:03:57.334 --> 00:04:00.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:03.234
Maybe a correction
Seth can't tell?

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:05.501
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.501 --> 00:04:07.968
We said -- We showed a picture
of Bernie Sanders,

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:11.100
um, in "Star Wars."

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.767
And we --
we called it a cruiser.

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:16.400
We should have said --
uh, "Star Wars" --

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.934
We should have said
an X4 Landspeeder.

00:04:18.934 --> 00:04:21.968
It's alright, nerds. Um...

00:04:21.968 --> 00:04:25.300
[ Laughter ]

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:29.934
Talked about
President Biden's train trip.

00:04:29.934 --> 00:04:33.534
Secret train trip
to Ukraine from Poland.

00:04:33.534 --> 00:04:35.601
Showed a stock photo of a train.

00:04:35.601 --> 00:04:38.601
Many said it was a German train.

00:04:38.601 --> 00:04:41.200
I want to clear that up
right away,

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.033
because when it comes
to attention to detail,

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:46.634
I consider train enthusiasts
the knitting community on rails.

00:04:46.634 --> 00:04:47.767
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:49.634
Hey, speaking of
the knitting community,

00:04:49.634 --> 00:04:52.467
you guys remember
my famous sweater vest,

00:04:52.467 --> 00:04:55.133
knitted by
the great Patty Lyons?

00:04:55.133 --> 00:04:58.334
Well, you might also remember
this whole thing --

00:04:58.334 --> 00:05:03.467
whole "Corrections" universe
started with a Lego error,

00:05:03.467 --> 00:05:06.200
and somebody made this

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.234
to commemorate
what we're doing.

00:05:08.234 --> 00:05:09.634
Look at this. It is a --

00:05:09.634 --> 00:05:11.300
It is a version of me

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.000
in my sweater vest,
made made of Lego.

00:05:16.267 --> 00:05:17.434
Pretty impressive.

00:05:17.434 --> 00:05:20.000
And then they go --
and this goes with it.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:21.734
And you guys will probably
figure this out faster,

00:05:21.734 --> 00:05:25.868
because I -- I was like,
"Is this like a Landspeeder X4?"

00:05:25.868 --> 00:05:27.968
And then I realized...

00:05:27.968 --> 00:05:31.067
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:33.467
I think this is...

00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:36.133
[ Laughter ]

00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:38.934
Really...

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.801
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:45.434
So, that's nice.

00:05:45.434 --> 00:05:47.367
I'm going to put that
over here for now.

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:48.801
We are gonna have to
clean this up.

00:05:48.801 --> 00:05:50.234
We're gonna do, like, a --

00:05:50.234 --> 00:05:52.834
Yeah, we'll do, like, a, um --

00:05:52.834 --> 00:05:54.767
a March Madness thing --

00:05:54.767 --> 00:05:56.133
you know, like
Tournament of Things we did,

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:58.033
first year.
Very popular.

00:05:58.033 --> 00:06:01.100
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:02.968
We'll do, like, a --
You'll have to vote.

00:06:02.968 --> 00:06:04.567
If something stays,
something goes.

00:06:04.567 --> 00:06:06.000
And this is a mess right now.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.601
So you go over
to seacaptainpolls.com,

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:12.567
blow the dust off it, and, uh...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.567 --> 00:06:14.267
"Oh, hey, guys,

00:06:14.267 --> 00:06:16.534
is this a nice --
is this a nice gift?

00:06:16.534 --> 00:06:19.033
Is this something
that you would send --

00:06:19.033 --> 00:06:21.334
you would go out of your way
to send in the mail

00:06:21.334 --> 00:06:25.434
to a person who's made it clear,
a certain phobia he has

00:06:25.434 --> 00:06:29.334
about a former fast-food mascot?

00:06:29.334 --> 00:06:30.534
Here, can you give me
a little --

00:06:30.534 --> 00:06:32.634
Can we give me a candle here --
camera here?

00:06:32.634 --> 00:06:35.567
Excuse me. Just heads?

00:06:35.567 --> 00:06:37.567
[ Laughter ]

00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:39.968
Just to see -- Just...

00:06:39.968 --> 00:06:43.400
a hundred heads of this...

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.033
If you want to buy these
for a friend,

00:06:45.033 --> 00:06:48.000
just go on Amazon and
search for the devil's teeth.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.000
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.434 --> 00:06:57.434
Oh, last week,
while just discussing

00:06:57.434 --> 00:06:59.501
Bruce Springsteen's
album "Nebraska,"

00:06:59.501 --> 00:07:02.234
I suggested Springsteen
would never sing the song

00:07:02.234 --> 00:07:04.133
"Mansion on the Hill"
in concert.

00:07:04.133 --> 00:07:07.300
He has played that song
138 times in concert...

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:08.534
[ Laughter ]

00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:11.434
...including just
last month in Florida.

00:07:11.434 --> 00:07:13.534
A Springsteen fanatic said
I should have said

00:07:13.534 --> 00:07:17.334
"State Trooper" or "Used Cars,"
which are the least played

00:07:17.334 --> 00:07:20.834
Springsteen songs in concert --
38 times each.

00:07:20.834 --> 00:07:24.400
"Used Cars," of course.
Do you remember that song?

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:26.400
It's where he tries
to sell all the cars

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.334
he's mentioned in other songs.

00:07:28.334 --> 00:07:30.601
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.601 --> 00:07:33.400
Pink Cadillacs, '69 Chevy.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:35.801
[ Snickers ]

00:07:35.801 --> 00:07:38.167
[ Laughter ]

00:07:38.167 --> 00:07:39.834
If you're on a budget,

00:07:39.834 --> 00:07:42.567
we got some skeleton frames
of burned-out Chevrolets.

00:07:42.567 --> 00:07:45.601
[ Laughter ]

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.834
Someone asked,
what is my favorite

00:07:50.834 --> 00:07:52.934
Springsteen album
if it's not "Nebraska"?

00:07:52.934 --> 00:07:54.968
I don't know.

00:07:54.968 --> 00:07:57.601
It's either "Wild --"
"The Wild and the Innocent,"

00:07:57.601 --> 00:08:01.934
or "Born to Run."
Gun to my head, desert island,

00:08:01.934 --> 00:08:03.467
I can only bring one,
I don't know.

00:08:03.467 --> 00:08:05.000
I think I might have to bring
"Born to Run"

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.300
because "Thunder Road" is
my favorite Springsteen song.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:09.767
So I don't know.
It's certainly not "Nebraska."

00:08:09.767 --> 00:08:13.534
However, if I could only bring
one movie to a desert island,

00:08:13.534 --> 00:08:16.601
it would be "Nebraska"
starring Will Forte.

00:08:16.601 --> 00:08:18.267
[ Laughter ]

00:08:18.267 --> 00:08:20.801
Which you haven't seen --
if you haven't seen,

00:08:20.801 --> 00:08:23.634
is like a less-funny
"MacGruber."

00:08:23.634 --> 00:08:26.901
[ Laughter ]

00:08:26.901 --> 00:08:29.434
Or a more funny "MacGruber,"
depending on where you fall

00:08:29.434 --> 00:08:31.400
on "MacGruber."

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.767
Very polarizing film.

00:08:33.767 --> 00:08:36.434
[ Laughter ]

00:08:36.434 --> 00:08:38.667
People have been saying
recently --

00:08:38.667 --> 00:08:40.868
and I think it's true,
and I think it's a credit

00:08:40.868 --> 00:08:42.200
to people
who come every week --

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.067
that this would be impossible
to follow

00:08:44.067 --> 00:08:46.667
if you didn't start
from the beginning.

00:08:46.667 --> 00:08:49.067
And you know,
I do encourage everybody

00:08:49.067 --> 00:08:50.801
who's watching this
for the first time

00:08:50.801 --> 00:08:53.033
and, you know,
frustrated by the fact

00:08:53.033 --> 00:08:56.968
that they don't have a pause
or stop on their laptop...

00:08:56.968 --> 00:09:00.167
[ Laughter ]

00:09:00.167 --> 00:09:01.801
...on the phone with
their IT guy right now,

00:09:01.801 --> 00:09:04.300
and be like, "Won't [Bleep]
stop playing!"

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:07.267
[ Laughter ]

00:09:07.267 --> 00:09:09.033
So I was thinking, like,
"Should we do it?

00:09:09.033 --> 00:09:10.934
Because we're coming up on 75.

00:09:10.934 --> 00:09:12.501
We're not gonna do
a big spectacular.

00:09:12.501 --> 00:09:15.000
But should we start
releasing them

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.667
from the beginning,
as a podcast?"

00:09:16.667 --> 00:09:18.467
You know, would that be
a helpful way

00:09:18.467 --> 00:09:20.267
for people to catch up?

00:09:20.267 --> 00:09:22.968
So I guess my question
for Leiderman would be,

00:09:22.968 --> 00:09:26.300
if you go connect
with the NBC podcast people

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:27.968
and then get back to me,

00:09:27.968 --> 00:09:29.000
do you think
we could get an answer

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:31.267
in like 10 months?

00:09:31.267 --> 00:09:33.868
[ Laughter ]

00:09:33.868 --> 00:09:36.801
Also, should we start --
I think, you know,

00:09:36.801 --> 00:09:38.667
we've done this
for so many years now,

00:09:38.667 --> 00:09:40.801
the dates are less helpful.

00:09:40.801 --> 00:09:43.767
Like, maybe we should,

00:09:43.767 --> 00:09:46.834
in the YouTube description,
put episode number.

00:09:46.834 --> 00:09:48.634
You can still put the date,
but I think maybe

00:09:48.634 --> 00:09:50.200
the episode number
would be helpful.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:51.367
And you might be saying,
"Hey, Seth,

00:09:51.367 --> 00:09:53.000
isn't this a conversation
you should be having

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.067
with Leiderman
during the workday?

00:09:55.067 --> 00:09:56.801
Why are you doing it
in the body of 'Corrections'?"

00:09:56.801 --> 00:09:59.200
Well, I want to do it like this.
No follow-ups.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:02.534
[ Laughter ]

00:10:10.968 --> 00:10:13.968
Popes can have kids.

00:10:13.968 --> 00:10:15.501
The pope's gonna have kids,
you guys.

00:10:15.501 --> 00:10:18.701
Alexander VI had
at least seven.

00:10:18.701 --> 00:10:21.200
Popes are allowed to have --
I did not realize --

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:25.067
as many kids as they want
under the ecclesiastical canon.

00:10:25.067 --> 00:10:28.267
The specific one
that allows them

00:10:28.267 --> 00:10:29.801
to have as many kids
as they want

00:10:29.801 --> 00:10:32.167
is the Nick Cannon.

00:10:32.167 --> 00:10:35.200
[ Laughter ]

00:10:38.634 --> 00:10:40.767
I mean, it's the sound
of a puzzle piece

00:10:40.767 --> 00:10:42.567
fitting perfectly, you know?

00:10:42.567 --> 00:10:44.400
Don't go looking for them.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.701
Sometimes they just
come and find me.

00:10:46.701 --> 00:10:50.400
[ Laughter ]

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.067
And of course, popes can
physically have children.

00:10:53.067 --> 00:10:54.801
You know?

00:10:54.801 --> 00:10:58.067
unless they have
a medical procedure

00:10:58.067 --> 00:11:01.434
known in the Catholic faith
as a Vatican-ectomy.

00:11:01.434 --> 00:11:05.033
[ Laughter ]

00:11:05.033 --> 00:11:07.267
I told you.

00:11:07.267 --> 00:11:09.534
[ Laughter ]

00:11:09.534 --> 00:11:11.033
[ Sighs ]

00:11:11.033 --> 00:11:13.334
It's just they were, like,
all terrible puns this week.

00:11:13.334 --> 00:11:15.133
[ Laughter ]

00:11:15.133 --> 00:11:17.934
That's not what I set out to do,
but then....

00:11:17.934 --> 00:11:20.200
Well, this one's not.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:23.200
We said Bernie Sanders' finger
was like a samurai sword.

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.267
And then we also said
he kept it in a holster.

00:11:25.267 --> 00:11:27.767
"If it was a sword,
it would be kept in a scabbard,

00:11:27.767 --> 00:11:29.367
a sheath, or a saya,

00:11:29.367 --> 00:11:31.267
which is the Japanese word
for 'sheath.'"

00:11:31.267 --> 00:11:33.400
And if you forget it,
it's easy to remember

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:34.901
because if you encounter
a samurai

00:11:34.901 --> 00:11:36.334
and they put their sword away,

00:11:36.334 --> 00:11:39.167
you breathe a [Inhales deeply]
sigh o' relief.

00:11:39.167 --> 00:11:42.200
[ Laughter ]

00:11:45.367 --> 00:11:47.868
It cost -- It cost us all
a little bit,

00:11:47.868 --> 00:11:49.934
but you won't forget it.

00:11:49.934 --> 00:11:52.167
[ Laughter ]

00:11:55.234 --> 00:11:57.033
It's time for "Animal Flubs."

00:11:57.033 --> 00:11:59.067
[ Laughter ]

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:04.534
Are you guys
not gonna do the song?

00:12:04.534 --> 00:12:06.767
-Uh, no.

00:12:06.767 --> 00:12:11.000
We're, uh, not gonna
sing the song today,

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:15.267
because I read
the YouTube comments,

00:12:15.267 --> 00:12:18.601
and someone wrote, quote,

00:12:18.601 --> 00:12:22.133
"I gotta say,
the 'Animal Flub' song

00:12:22.133 --> 00:12:24.300
[Bleep] sucks.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.300
It's not funny.

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:29.534
And not in the anti-humor
kind of way.

00:12:29.534 --> 00:12:33.634
It's grating and it's boring,"
end quote.

00:12:33.634 --> 00:12:36.267
-What a son of a aaah!

00:12:36.267 --> 00:12:38.367
Ech.

00:12:38.367 --> 00:12:41.534
[ Laughter ]

00:12:41.534 --> 00:12:45.234
We joked that someone

00:12:45.234 --> 00:12:50.667
drove, uh -- crashed their car
into a drive-through

00:12:50.667 --> 00:12:54.367
because they were
out of biscuits.

00:12:54.367 --> 00:12:57.367
And that they needed
the biscuits

00:12:57.367 --> 00:12:59.901
because it takes the edge
off the meth.

00:12:59.901 --> 00:13:02.701
But someone said
that doesn't track,

00:13:02.701 --> 00:13:05.300
because meth is
an appetite suppressant.

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:08.567
So you wouldn't take it and
then want biscuits that badly.

00:13:08.567 --> 00:13:11.667
And actually meth
is taking advantage of this.

00:13:11.667 --> 00:13:13.634
They're rebranding themselves,
Shoemaker.

00:13:13.634 --> 00:13:15.000
[ Laughter ]

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:16.767
You've probably seen the ads.

00:13:16.767 --> 00:13:19.734
"Meth -- the poor man's
Ozempic."

00:13:19.734 --> 00:13:23.200
[ Laughter ]

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:25.467
Alright.

00:13:25.467 --> 00:13:26.868
Last thing --

00:13:26.868 --> 00:13:30.100
and this is for our friends
over at YouTube,

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:32.834
because we don't do
the closed captioning.

00:13:32.834 --> 00:13:34.234
Okay?

00:13:34.234 --> 00:13:36.133
So you can criticize
the closed captioning

00:13:36.133 --> 00:13:37.734
all you want.
We have nothing to do with it.

00:13:37.734 --> 00:13:39.767
I do appreciate
that YouTube does it.

00:13:39.767 --> 00:13:42.534
Very happy that people who rely
on closed captioning have it.

00:13:42.534 --> 00:13:45.300
But sometimes I need you
to make some editorial decisions

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:49.033
and think before you put it in

00:13:49.033 --> 00:13:51.300
how it's going to look
to the viewer.

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:54.167
Now, last week
I'm wearing mosquito headpiece.

00:13:54.167 --> 00:13:56.534
I didn't know ahead of time
that the antenna

00:13:56.534 --> 00:13:58.167
we're gonna be falling
into my face.

00:13:58.167 --> 00:14:02.634
So, out of necessity,
I'm blowing them out of my face.

00:14:02.634 --> 00:14:04.567
Like, that's not a thing, right?

00:14:04.567 --> 00:14:07.968
Like, even if
you are hearing-impaired,

00:14:07.968 --> 00:14:09.767
you can tell that
I'm blowing, right?

00:14:09.767 --> 00:14:11.901
There's no reason
to put that in.

00:14:11.901 --> 00:14:13.501
Because here's the problem.

00:14:13.501 --> 00:14:16.834
It's just a picture of me,
and it says "blows."

00:14:16.834 --> 00:14:19.934
[ Laughter ]

00:14:24.801 --> 00:14:26.601
Who's that for? You know?

00:14:26.601 --> 00:14:29.501
Like, just I think,
in general, like,

00:14:29.501 --> 00:14:31.667
let's all just do better.

00:14:31.667 --> 00:14:34.167
[ Laughter ]

00:14:34.167 --> 00:14:37.067
We've done this --
We've done like 74 of these.

00:14:39.067 --> 00:14:41.300
[ Laughter ]

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:45.067
Yeah, and some weeks,
I don't have an out.

00:14:45.067 --> 00:14:48.267
[ Laughter ]

00:14:48.267 --> 00:14:50.634
See me next week.

